.TH ZPOOLPILOT 8 "November 2026" "ZpoolPilot 0.1.0-alpha.1" "System Administration"
.SH STATUS
Alpha source. Limited loop-backed VM and service validation is recorded in
/usr/share/doc/zpoolpilot/testing.md. Boot-recovery and hardware validation
remain pending. Storage-changing workflows are not ready for production use.
.SH NAME
zpoolpilot \- safety-focused OpenZFS monitoring and storage operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zpoolpilot
.RI [ global-options ]
.RI [ command ]
.PP
.B zpoolpilot plan
.I operation
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B sudo zpoolpilot apply
.RI [ --command-timeout " duration" ]
.I plan.json
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zpoolpilot
collects OpenZFS, block-device, udev, SMART, ARC, and device-link information
into a versioned snapshot. It provides a color-coded terminal dashboard,
line-oriented and JSON status, stable drive/bay inventory, and a cached
Prometheus exporter.
.PP
Supported changes are represented by typed, inspectable plans with a maximum
15-minute lifetime. Applying a plan requires root, a real terminal, a fresh
safety snapshot before every command, interactive confirmation, and
before/after audit records. There is no unattended assume-yes option.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --config " path"
Read configuration from
.I path
instead of /etc/zpoolpilot/config.toml. A root invocation accepts only a
root-owned, singly linked regular config whose complete directory ancestor
chain is root-owned, nonsymlinked, and not group/other-writable; configured
writes remain inside their dedicated application subtrees.
.TP
.B --read-only
Install the observer-only command policy and reject planning, apply, bay/LED
changes, and deliberate disk wake-up.
.TP
.BR --color =auto|always|never
Control ANSI color. The
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable also disables color.
.TP
.BR --version ", " -V
Print build version information.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B tui [--refresh]
Open the full-screen dashboard (the default command). Pages cover overview,
pools/vdevs, drives/bays, datasets/settings, ARC/performance, tasks, actions,
and command trace. Arrow keys or p/n change page, j/k scroll, 1-8 jump, r
refreshes read-only data, ? opens help, and q quits. The Actions page explains
commands but never mutates storage.
.TP
.B status [--json] [--refresh] [--include-commands]
Show the cached status, or collect a fresh no-wake snapshot. Command traces
contain argv and status metadata, not captured output or interactive secrets.
.TP
.B inventory scan [--json] [--wake]
Collect drive identity, make/model, serial, capacity, type/transport, use, and
bay data and update the cache. Without
.B --wake
SMART uses guarded ATA/SATA collection and explicit NVMe access; other
transports require an explicit --wake refresh.
.TP
.B inventory list [--json]
List drives from the cache.
.TP
.B inventory mappings [--json]
List every authoritative manual mapping in deterministic drive-ID order,
including mappings whose old drive is currently absent. A missing mapping file
is reported as an empty list; malformed or untrusted state is an error.
.TP
.B inventory map --drive ID --bay LABEL [--enclosure NAME] [--slot N]
Persist a tentative manual bay mapping for the stable drive identity. It has
inferred confidence and no verification timestamp. Root, a real terminal, and
yellow confirmation are required. The slot may be omitted; explicit slot zero
is retained as a known slot.
.TP
.B inventory verify --drive ID [--bay LABEL] [--enclosure NAME] [--slot N]
After a physical check, promote the supplied or currently saved values to a
manual verified mapping with a timestamp. The explicitly supplied or inherited
slot must be known. A canonical live type-13 SES character device must expose a
reliable udev WWN or hardware serial, which is pinned as enclosure identity.
.TP
.B inventory unmap --drive EXACT_STORED_ID
Retire one authoritative mapping so a replacement drive can reuse its physical
bay. The argument must exactly match a stored wwn: or serial: key shown by
.BR "inventory mappings" ;
aliases and devnodes are not resolved, and the old disk need not be present.
Root, a real terminal, and yellow confirmation are required. After the prompt,
the mapping is reloaded and compare-and-deleted under the writer lock only if
the complete stored record is unchanged. Clear any active identify LED before
unmapping; deletion intentionally removes the slot authority needed to clear it.
.TP
.B inventory locate --drive ID [--state on|off]
Set or clear a supported SES identify LED. This requires a manually verified
mapping with timestamp, nonempty label, numeric slot, and an existing direct
character device at canonical /dev/sgN or /dev/bsg/H:C:T:L. Inferred mappings
and symlinks are refused. Enclosure identity is checked before confirmation and
again immediately before dispatch. Turning the LED on requires a present,
strongly identified drive before and after confirmation and physical
verification no more than 15 minutes old. Turning it off accepts only the exact
stored key shown by inventory mappings, but permits an absent drive and older
verification so an orphaned indicator can be cleared. A timestamp more than one
minute ahead is refused for both states. Root and a real terminal are required.
The trusted mapping is reloaded and compared after confirmation. The command uses sg_ses
--dev-slot-num=N.
.PP
Map, verify, unmap, and locate each require root, a real terminal, and yellow
confirmation. Map, verify, and locate-on also require fresh fast no-wake safety
discovery; locate-off and unmap intentionally use the exact stored key because
the retired drive may be absent. Enclosure identity comes from a
non-placeholder udev WWN or SCSI serial after proving a live SCSI type-13
enclosure-services device; it is not inferred from the path or a direct SES
logical-ID query. Map and verify repeat exact drive fingerprint/major:minor
discovery after confirmation; verify also repeats the enclosure probe before
storing.
Always physically match the bay label and drive serial. Never pull a disk
solely because its identify LED is lit.
.TP
.B plan OPERATION
Perform fresh non-waking safety discovery, show exact commands and drive
details, and save an owner-only JSON plan. Blocked plans are saved for
inspection but cannot be applied. Every operation accepts \fB--out\fR
\fIpath\fR. An ordinary user defaults to the current directory; a root
invocation defaults to and confines output beneath the configured plan
directory.
.TP
.B apply [--command-timeout DURATION] PLAN.json
Validate, lock, revalidate, confirm, and execute an unexpired plan. Must run as
root in a trusted terminal.
.TP
.B monitor collect [--profile fast|hardware|all] [--wait] [--wake] [--include-commands]
Collect and atomically persist a snapshot. Fast collection skips SMART while
preserving matching cached hardware data. Hardware/all collection is no-wake
unless the interactive yellow
.B --wake
option is supplied.
.TP
.B monitor serve [--listen ADDRESS] [--max-age DURATION]
Serve the cached snapshot at /metrics, /healthz, and /readyz. The default
address is 127.0.0.1:9815. Readiness requires a fresh, non-partial snapshot.
Failed metric sources remain visible as source errors while their affected
numeric samples are omitted; successful compatibility-fallback measurements
remain available. No hardware command runs on scrape.
.TP
.B doctor [--json]
Report required and optional runtime capabilities.
.SH PLAN OPERATIONS
.SS Red disk and topology operations
.TP
.B create-pool --pool NAME --topology TYPE --drives ID[,ID...] [options]
Create a whole-disk single, mirror, raidz1, raidz2, or raidz3 pool. Options
include repeatable
.BR --property " and " --dataset-property ,
.BR --profile =general|proxmox,
.BR --expert ,
and native encryption by passphrase or existing secured key file. Key files
accept
.BR --key-format =passphrase|raw|hex.
The single topology accepts exactly one disk.
.TP
.B prepare-disk --drive ID
Remove partition-table and eligible inactive filesystem metadata. It is not a
secure erase. Mounted, swap, LSBLK/sysfs holder, active ZFS, root, and boot
devices block. Raw ZFS/LVM/MD/LUKS/Ceph/BitLocker/swap ownership signatures
also block even when unmounted because their external owner is not proven
inactive.
.TP
.B replace-disk --pool POOL --vdev VDEV --drive ID
Replace a data-pool leaf with a sufficiently large unused drive. The selected
vdev must report a nonzero authoritative size.
.TP
.B attach-mirror --pool POOL --vdev VDEV --drive ID
Attach a leaf to form or widen a mirror. The selected vdev must report a
nonzero authoritative size.
.TP
.B expand-raidz --pool POOL --vdev VDEV --drive ID
Add one drive to a top-level RAIDZ vdev. Every child must report a nonzero
authoritative size. OpenZFS 2.3 or newer is required.
.TP
.B add-vdev --pool POOL --topology TYPE --drives ID[,ID...]
Add one top-level data vdev matching the existing data topology.
.TP
.B add-spare --pool POOL --drives ID[,ID...]
Add one or more unused whole disks as hot spares. Every target must be at least
as large as the pool's largest data leaf; unknown data-leaf size blocks.
.TP
.B replace-boot-disk --pool POOL --vdev OLD --source-drive HEALTHY --drive NEW --boot-mode systemd-boot|grub
Run the guarded Proxmox boot replacement described below.
.SS Yellow routine maintenance
.TP
.B scrub-start|scrub-stop|trim-start|trim-stop|clear-errors --pool POOL
Create a yellow plan for exactly one
.BR "zpool scrub" ,
.BR "zpool scrub -s" ,
.BR "zpool trim" ,
.BR "zpool trim -s" ,
or
.B zpool clear
command. Scrub start is blocked by active or unknown scan state, active or
suspended manual TRIM, and unknown or uncertain mixed TRIM state. Scrub stop
requires a confirmed active scrub. Despite its CLI name, trim-stop suspends a
consistently active pass; it does not cancel it. Trim start begins an
untrimmed/complete/canceled pass or resumes a consistently suspended pass.
Unknown, mixed, or unsupported TRIM state blocks start. The pool must be
ONLINE or DEGRADED. Boot pools are allowed with a latency/availability warning.
.SH SAFETY
Disk plans pin stable hardware identity, /dev/disk/by-id path, capacity,
sector geometry, major:minor number, host identity, and pool topology.
/dev/sdX alone is never authority. Group/other-writable, symlink, oversized,
expired, structurally unknown, or edited plan files are rejected. A plan ID is
exactly 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters.
.PP
Green operations run automatically. Yellow commands display exact argv and
accept Enter, y, or yes at [Y/n]. Red commands require the exact
.B yes SERIAL
or
.B yes FINGERPRINT
challenge; Enter alone never approves them. External commands are dispatched
as argv arrays without a shell. Noninteractive capture is limited to 64 MiB of
stdout and 1 MiB of stderr; overflow fails the command.
.PP
Read-only monitoring can use legacy OpenZFS text output as a best-effort
fallback. Storage planning requires complete safety-authority discovery,
including the supported OpenZFS JSON interfaces, and fails closed otherwise.
Pool status/list/get JSON must be structurally complete and agree exactly on
pool name/GUID coverage; bootfs, root-dataset mountpoint, leaf-path, and scan
authority cannot silently disappear. Unknown scan/TRIM authority blocks red
lifecycle operations, and pool creation checks every imported pool.
.PP
The actual / entry in /proc/self/mountinfo is independent boot evidence. A ZFS
root marks its source pool boot-critical even with bootfs=none or
mountpoint=legacy. Missing, malformed, ambiguous, or unmatched root-mount
evidence makes safety discovery partial and blocks planning.
.PP
Replace and attach require authoritative nonzero selected-vdev size. RAIDZ
expansion requires it for every child. Fresh apply discovery rechecks these
capacity inputs before dispatch. Adding spares likewise rechecks complete data-
leaf size authority and every target against the largest data leaf.
.PP
Immediately before each red command, a live guard requires every command-line
by-id disk link to remain a symlink to the pinned block-device major:minor and
sysfs whole disk. Terminal output replaces untrusted escape/C0/C1 and Unicode
directional controls; ANSI styling is generated internally.
.PP
Plans that consume a disk as a new pool member collect SMART in no-wake all
mode. Known failed/critical target health blocks planning and is rechecked
immediately before dispatch. Missing, sleeping, or unsupported SMART remains a
prominent warning, not a healthy result. prepare-disk remains available for
retiring failed media; routine maintenance uses fast discovery.
.SH DRIVE AND SMART SEMANTICS
/dev/disk/by-path provides an inferred controller-path label, not proof of a
physical chassis bay. Manual inferred and verified mappings overlay cached
discovery; only verify records physical confirmation and its timestamp. Unknown
bays remain visible and duplicate mappings are critical findings. Use
.B inventory mappings
to discover an absent retired drive's exact key. If its identify LED may be
active, use that key with
.B inventory locate --state off
first; then use
.B inventory unmap
before assigning the same bay to its replacement.
.PP
When persisting a snapshot, the fast collector preserves old SMART values only
when stable ID, WWN, serial, and capacity still match. Hardware collection uses
.B smartctl --device sat --nocheck standby,2,2
for ATA/SATA and an explicit nvme device type for NVMe.
Unsupported/unknown ATA power checks stop before SMART reads.
SAS/SCSI, USB bridges, and unknown transports are skipped and shown as unknown
unless an operator authorizes an interactive --wake refresh. If a drive sleeps, prior values retain their original timestamp
and are marked sleeping. Never-collected data and data older than twice the
configured hardware interval are unknown. Missing firmware fields are absent,
not zero.
Persisted collectors hold the shared lock from discovery through cache writes.
Failed or canceled collection preserves the previous snapshot. Dashboard
refreshes retain matching cached hardware readings and reevaluate their age;
the exporter reevaluates health age and labels on each scrape.
.PP
A valid NVMe percentage_used field is authoritative for wear, including values
above 100 percent. Corrected SCSI errors are not uncorrected media errors.
ATA wear accepts
only allowlisted normalized top-level values from 0 through 100: remaining-life
values are converted to used life, while direct used-life values are retained.
Raw, invalid, or disagreeing ATA values remain unknown.
.PP
A SMART timestamp more than one minute ahead produces an unknown finding.
Persisted JSON snapshots with missing/far-future generation time or far-future
SMART time are rejected and make /readyz return 503. Database-fallback
snapshots receive the same timestamp validation, and invalid values from
either cache do not feed telemetry carry-forward. Both cache forms require the
supported SnapshotV1 API version.
.PP
Status JSON and the TUI report manual TRIM support/state/progress at leaf and
pool level when OpenZFS provides it. Incomplete or disagreeing leaf state is
reported as unknown or mixed rather than guessed.
.SH PROMETHEUS
Metrics cover host/OpenZFS/Proxmox boot mode and snapshot severity/source
errors; pool state, space, fragmentation, I/O errors and rates, scan and manual
TRIM progress; recursive vdev state, errors and TRIM; dataset space,
compression ratio, and selected setting/source labels; drive
identity/bay/capacity/use, SMART, temperature, wear and error counters;
ARC/L2ARC; and findings.
.PP
Raw pool free space and dataset available are distinct. Fragmentation is an
allocator metric, not disk damage. Encryption key locations are excluded from
metric labels.
.SH PROXMOX BOOT REPLACEMENT
The dedicated boot plan requires detected Proxmox host evidence, one consistent
mode from
.BR "proxmox-boot-tool status" ,
a boot-critical mirrored pool using persistent partition 3, one healthy online
source sibling whole disk, and a clean, distinct, same-size-or-larger target.
Source and target must have matching logical sector sizes.
Plain grub-install layouts are unsupported.
.PP
Apply clones the source partition table to the target, randomizes target GUIDs,
runs partprobe, formats and initializes target partition 2 with
proxmox-boot-tool, then performs zpool replace using target partition 3.
After partprobe, the live guard also proves that the partition-2 and
partition-3 links are block devices owned by the exact planned whole disk.
It does not repair existing ESP state, convert boot mode, wait for resilvering,
or validate a reboot. Verify zpool and Proxmox boot status and conduct a
controlled reboot test afterward.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/zpoolpilot/config.toml
Configuration. Unknown keys and invalid values are rejected. Under root it
must be root-owned and not group/other-writable; its entire directory ancestor
chain must meet the same ownership and write rules and contain no symlink.
.TP
.I /etc/zpoolpilot/bays.json
Versioned authoritative manual bay mappings. The root-owned, singly linked,
bounded regular file is locked and atomically replaced by map/verify/unmap; monitoring
services may read but not write it. Installation seeds an empty v1 file and
preserves it on reinstall. A malformed/untrusted file produces partial
inventory.bays/trust status. Legacy state.db bay rows are ignored and must be
remapped and physically verified.
.TP
.I /var/lib/zpoolpilot/latest.json
Atomic monitoring snapshot cache.
.TP
.I /var/lib/zpoolpilot/state.db
Shared SQLite snapshot cache state; never bay or mutation-audit authority. If
sqlite3 is unavailable, the atomic JSON fallback is state.db.json.
.TP
.I /var/log/zpoolpilot/audit.jsonl
Authoritative root-owned, root-writable JSONL before/after records for plan
application attempts.
.TP
.I /run/zpoolpilot/apply.lock
Exclusive plan-application lock.
.TP
.I /run/zpoolpilot/collect/collect.lock
Shared persisted-collection and snapshot-writer lock.
.TP
.I /run/zpoolpilot/plans/
Packaged short-lived plan directory used and enforced when plan creation runs
as root. An ordinary user defaults to the current directory unless --out is
supplied.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Healthy status/persisted snapshot, or successful command.
.TP
.B 1
Warning health; also used for an interactive decline and selected general
command failures.
.TP
.B 2
Critical health; also used for a blocked/invalid plan, failed apply, unresolved
target, or missing required doctor capability.
.TP
.B 3
Unknown/partial health or incomplete safety authority.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command usage or arguments.
.TP
.B 69
Collection did not complete after snapshot persistence.
.TP
.B 74
Snapshot lock or persistence failed. Unlike health exits 1-3, packaged
collector units treat this as failure.
.TP
.B 77
Denied by read-only mode, missing apply or bay-change privilege/TTY, or another
policy gate.
.TP
.B 78
Configuration loading or validation failed.
.TP
.B 130
Cancelled by signal.
.PP
Packaged collector units declare health exits 1, 2, and 3 successful so systemd
records the health state without reporting an operational service failure.
.SH PRIVILEGE MODEL
The packaged fast collector and exporter run as the unprivileged
.B zpoolpilot
account. The no-wake hardware collector is a short-lived root oneshot that
still uses
.BR --read-only .
The exporter has no device namespace or capabilities. No service can apply a
plan; only a foreground operator can cross the mutation boundary. Shared cache
state is writable by the collectors, while the audit log is not writable by
the monitoring account.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Automatic SES slot discovery, Proxmox storage registration, remote control,
alerts, boot-mode conversion, and dRAID/log/special/cache-vdev lifecycle
changes are not implemented. Existing auxiliary vdevs can be displayed.
Physical hot-swap, SES, HBA, and boot/reboot behavior require validation on
representative disposable hardware or virtual machines.
